WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.856
Data sovereignty.

00:00:01.806 --> 00:00:04.772
Your data, their servers, foreign laws.

00:00:04.772 --> 00:00:14.329
The Canadian government stores citizen data on cloud infrastructure operated by American corporations: Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.

00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:26.897
These US companies are subject to the US CLOUD Act, which allows US law enforcement and intelligence agencies to compel disclosure of data stored by US companies — regardless of where in the world that data is physically located.

00:00:26.897 --> 00:00:35.352
This means that Canadian government data stored on AWS servers in Canada is still potentially accessible to US intelligence under US law.

00:00:35.352 --> 00:00:45.238
The Five Eyes intelligence alliance further complicates data sovereignty — Canada and the US share intelligence under arrangements that operate largely outside parliamentary oversight.

00:00:45.238 --> 00:00:53.045
When your government stores your data on infrastructure controlled by foreign corporations under foreign law, data sovereignty is not a technical issue.

00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:59.374
It is a question of whether your most sensitive information is ultimately subject to Canadian law or American law.

00:00:59.374 --> 00:01:02.295
Three layers of the data sovereignty problem.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.443
The digital sovereignty gap.

00:01:04.443 --> 00:01:12.852
Data sovereignty connects the digital identity infrastructure, the national security state, and the institutional capture architecture into a single system.

00:01:12.852 --> 00:01:16.795
Your digital identity is built on WEF frameworks.

00:01:16.795 --> 00:01:20.386
Your data is stored on US cloud infrastructure.

00:01:20.386 --> 00:01:23.545
That data is accessible under US law.

00:01:23.545 --> 00:01:26.204
It is shared through Five Eyes channels.

00:01:26.204 --> 00:01:32.568
The oversight bodies that should protect your data — the Privacy Commissioner, NSICOP, NSIRA — cannot enforce.

00:01:32.568 --> 00:01:35.170
Your government chose this architecture.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:39.159
It chose US cloud providers over sovereign infrastructure.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:43.079
It chose Five Eyes intelligence sharing over data protection.

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:48.034
It chose WEF digital identity frameworks over domestic alternatives.

00:01:48.034 --> 00:01:52.181
Each choice serves institutional alignment over citizen privacy.

00:01:52.181 --> 00:01:56.715
This is data sovereignty surrendered — not lost, but deliberately given away.
